DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ccrryypptt reads the standard input, enciphers or deciphers it
     under _p_a_s_s_w_o_r_d, and writes the result to the standard out-
     put.  The cipher is symmetric, so running ccrryypptt a second
     time over the output, with the same _p_a_s_s_w_o_r_d, recovers the
     original text.

     If _p_a_s_s_w_o_r_d is not given as an argument, ccrryypptt prompts for
     it at the terminal without echoing what is typed.  An empty
     password typed at the prompt is rejected; only the first 10
     characters of _p_a_s_s_w_o_r_d are significant.  Once the password
     has been copied out of the argument vector (or out of the
     prompt buffer), that copy is overwritten with nulls so the
     plaintext password does not linger in memory.

     Internally, ccrryypptt pads the password to 10 characters with a
     fixed built-in key, forces the last two characters of the
     padded key into the range legal for a password `salt', and
     passes the result through the system password-hashing
     routine ccrryypptt(). The last 11 characters of that hash become
     the working key.  The first 6 characters of the working key
     seed a pseudo-random shuffle of a 256-byte substitution
     table (a self-inverse `rotor'); the last 6 characters seed a
     second pseudo-random sequence that supplies a running of-
     fset.  Each byte read from the standard input is combined
     with the next offset from that sequence, passed through the
     rotor, and the result is written to the standard output.

